<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">What this guide covers</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    This guide is a sequence of steps for making your pages eligible for AI answers, useful enough to be cited, and measurable once they are. It deliberately leaves out the background on how assistants choose passages, which our <a href="/resources/answer-engine-optimization-whitepaper/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">answer engine optimization whitepaper</a> covers. Here the aim is a working order of tasks.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    One caution first. No one can promise a citation. The official guidance is short, and much of what circulates about AI visibility comes from tool vendors. Each step below says where it comes from.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 1. Make sure the page can be crawled and indexed</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Google states that to be used in AI Overviews or AI Mode, "a page must be indexed and eligible to be shown in Google Search with a snippet." It adds that there are no additional requirements. So start with the basics: check crawling is allowed in robots.txt and by any CDN or hosting layer, and confirm in Google Search Console that the page is indexed.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    For Microsoft Copilot, the equivalent check is Bing Webmaster Tools. Microsoft says Bing honours content owner preferences expressed through robots.txt and other supported controls, so a block there will also keep content out.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 2. Keep important content as text</h2>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Google lists "making sure that important content is available in textual form" among its recommendations. Put facts, definitions, prices and steps in the page text, not only in images, scripts or downloads. Support the text with images and video where they help.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 3. Lead with a plain answer</h2>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Microsoft's guidance for publishers recommends clear headings, tables and FAQs. Our own suggestion, not a finding from the sources: start each page or section with a one or two sentence answer that makes sense on its own, then give the detail. Use headings that match the question a person would ask.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 4. Add depth, evidence and original detail</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Microsoft recommends deepening expertise on core topics and supporting claims with evidence. In practice that means naming the source of each figure, linking to the primary document, giving dates, and saying what you did not check. Pages that only restate common knowledge give an assistant no reason to prefer yours.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Our own suggestion: include something a summary cannot replace, such as a worked example, a dataset you hold, a table or a method you used.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 5. Match structured data to the page, and skip the tricks</h2>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Google says to make sure "your structured data matches the visible text on the page" and that there is "no special schema.org structured data that you need to add." Use Article, Organization or FAQ markup where it describes real content, and do not add markup for content a visitor cannot see. Be wary of any product that promises a special route into Google AI features that Google says does not exist.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 6. Keep content current and tell search engines</h2>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Microsoft recommends keeping content up to date and using IndexNow so search engines learn about changes and AI systems reference current versions. Show a last-updated date only when you have actually reviewed the page.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 7. Make your profiles consistent with your site</h2>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    A study by Yext Research of 17.2 million AI citations, reported in September 2026, found that brand-owned sites supplied 46.89% of citations and managed third-party listings 43.71%. The split varied by model. Yext sells listing management, so treat the percentages as directional (<a href="https://www.yext.com/blog/chatgpt-make-your-brand-visible-ai-search" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Yext blog post</a>). The practical step is cheap: make the description, services and location on your directory and profile pages match your website. We discuss the study further in <a href="/blog/where-ai-answers-get-their-sources/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">where AI answers get their sources</a>.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 8. Measure citations and clicks separately</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Measure in two places, because they answer different questions.
  </p>
  <ul class="mb-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li><strong>Microsoft Copilot citations.</strong> The Bing Webmaster Tools AI Performance report shows total citations, average cited pages per day, citations by page and a sample of grounding queries. Microsoft notes that the data is a sample, and that page counts do not show ranking, authority or placement within an answer. The report does not cover clicks.</li>
    <li><strong>Google AI features.</strong> Google says traffic from AI features is included in the overall Search Console Performance report under the Web search type. It is not broken out separately, so compare pages and queries over time rather than looking for an AI filter.</li>
    <li><strong>Outcomes.</strong> Use your analytics for conversions and engaged time, since neither report tells you whether a visitor became a customer.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    For what Google says about clicks, see <a href="/blog/ai-overviews-fewer-clicks-what-google-says-to-do/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">AI Overviews and fewer clicks</a>.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 9. Match page type to the question</h2>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Our own suggestion, not a finding from the sources: a deep technical article is a good source for a research question, but it will not answer a buyer who is comparing suppliers. Decide which questions you want to be cited for, then build a page designed for each: explainers for learning questions, and clear service, pricing and evidence pages for buying questions. Our report shows why this matters for us.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Checklist</h2>
  <ul class="mb-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li>The page is indexed in Google Search Console and visible in Bing Webmaster Tools.</li>
    <li>robots.txt, CDN and hosting allow crawling.</li>
    <li>Important facts are in the page text.</li>
    <li>The first lines of each section answer the question directly.</li>
    <li>Every figure names its primary source, with a date.</li>
    <li>The page holds something original: data, an example, a method.</li>
    <li>Structured data matches the visible text, with no hidden claims.</li>
    <li>IndexNow or a sitemap is set up so changes are noticed.</li>
    <li>Profiles and listings use the same description, services and location as your site.</li>
    <li>You review Bing citations, Search Console clicks and analytics conversions on a regular schedule.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    If you want help with an audit, see our <a href="/solutions/aeo-seo/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">answer engine optimization service</a> or <a href="/schedule-a-call/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">schedule a call</a>.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Sources</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
